  {
    "episode_id": 64559,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/4/64559.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Congressman, I have been fascinated by politics all my life. When I was eight years old, I had a squabble with my sister because on Sunday morning, I wanted to watch Meet the Press and she wanted to watch, I love Lucy reruns. So I've been doing this 50 years following in these little details and I noticed something last night because no matter who the president is, one of my favorite parts of the State of the Union address is when the Sergeant at Arms makes that announcement. And it'll sound silly when I say it, but when you hear it, I actually get, you know, not chills, but I get excited. Mr. President, Mr. Speaker, or in this case, Madam Speaker, the President of the United States, and I always think Oh, that's just a great moment. And both sides of the aisle applaud. Everyone is excited and unified in supporting the office. Even if they disagree with the individual, this is the president of the United States. It has always, always, always excited me. Last night, I noticed the strangest thing I have seen in all my years of watching the State of the Union. When the president came in, he turned to his left, and he worked his way down the aisle, and he very briefly turned around and shook hands with Kevin Brady, and I can't remember, there was one other Republican. He made a brief look to the right, and literally turned his back on the Republican side of the aisle, as he worked his way all the way down to the front. I've never, ever seen a president, no matter who, no matter how divided, ignore the other side.",
