    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/4/64559.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "I wrote a column in the Washington Times today and I called it the state of confusion address as opposed to the state of the union address. I just, there were so many disconnects and so many things. You mentioned, you know, the funds the police or secure the border. I'm thinking, great, please do, but where have you been for the last year? Because that's the polar opposite of what you've been saying. Let's shift gears. One of my pet peeves has been the way our capital city and Congress as a whole has handled or the leadership of Congress has handled mask mandates, all that goes along with vaccines, everything. God bless. I know we all want to try to be as healthy as we can. We all want to be considerate of other people that work or live around us. But I also think you have to leave to each individual some personal choice. The idea that I'm going to be poked or prodded or pushed into getting the vaccine, whether I want to or not, or else be penalized severely for it, I find really irritating. The latest version of this is the adults last night. of you were wearing a mask in Congress. If people don't know, that's the first time in a long time that you haven't been required to wear a mask if you go to the floor. But if you're a school kid in Washington, DC today, that's right. You're still wearing a mask.",
